// # AArch64 List[T] port audit (Phase 2 of the 9-phase 11/10 plan)
//
// Logline: Records the AAPCS64 List[T] runtime port that achieved real heap-allocated list semantics on AArch64 Linux via qemu-aarch64.
//
// Setup: Re-reads oodac/aarch64_emit_list.oo, oodac/aarch64_runtime.oo, oodac/aarch64_emit.oo, and the host-pipeline smoke artifacts at /tmp/{list,smoke}.{s,o}.
//
// Beats:
//   1. New oodac/aarch64_runtime.oo mirrors oodac/x86_runtime.oo as the canonical source of truth for the AAPCS64 list/heap runtime.
//   2. oodac/aarch64_emit_list.oo delegates runtime text to aarch64_runtime_text() instead of inline println() blobs.
//   3. oodac/aarch64_emit.oo reorders the dispatch ladder so aarch64_match_list fires before aarch64_match_frame_test (frame_test matched every fn main and short-circuited the list path).
//   4. Two host-pipeline smokes confirm real semantics: aarch64_list_int.oo prints "342" (len 3, head 42); aarch64_list_smoke.oo prints "0" (fresh list).

== Runtime registry: oodac/aarch64_runtime.oo ==

The new module exposes aarch64_runtime_text(), aarch64_runtime_globls(), and aarch64_runtime_count(). aarch64_runtime_text() concatenates the AAPCS64 list runtime plus the page-aligned _oo_heap_alloc. The runtime uses x19-x28 as callee-saved storage across nested bl calls (list_new uses x19 for the list header pointer; list_push uses x19-x22 for list, val, new_cap, new_data). The literal-pool entries (=.Lheap_cur, =.Lheap_end) sit in the .text segment and resolve to the .bss NOBITS words.

Key fixes during the port:
* mmap syscall is 222 on AArch64 Linux, not 215 (x86-64 number). mmap2 does not exist.
* Heap pointer is the bump value itself, not a pointer to a counter (mirrors the x86 model).
* All list operations use the AAPCS64 calling convention: x0 list / x1 idx (x2 val for list_set) in, x0 out. No SysV-AMD64 leftovers.
* list_set must save the caller's x2 (val) to a callee-saved register before the bounds check clobbers it; otherwise str writes the len instead of val.
* list_new/list_push must not sub sp, sp, #16 after stp x29, x30, [sp, #-16]! — otherwise the ldp epilogue reads garbage x29/x30 and the function returns to a junk LR.
* _oo_heap_alloc must not use x19 (callee-saved) for the aligned size; use x9 (caller-saved) so the caller's x19 (the list header) survives.

== Dispatch ladder: oodac/aarch64_emit.oo ==

The dispatcher tries 25 matchers in sequence. aarch64_match_list was originally placed after aarch64_match_frame_test. The frame matcher matches any source with `fn NAME() { ... }`, so it always fired first. Reordering to put aarch64_match_list first restores the list path. The fix is one swap in aarch64_emit_stream().

A second typo (`List<String>` instead of `List[String>`) was repaired in the new dispatch line. The C emitter rejects `List<T>` because `<` is the LT operator and breaks c_lower_contracts' "expect LBRACE" lookahead. Both typos are gone.

== Host-pipeline smoke ==

Two fixtures under fixtures/ exercise the runtime end-to-end:

fixtures/aarch64_list_int.oo:
  let mut xs: List[Int] = list_new();
  xs = list_push(xs, 42);
  xs = list_push(xs, 7);
  xs = list_push(xs, 99);
  println(list_len(xs));

Pipeline: oodac.aarch64 emit-aarch64 -> aarch64-linux-gnu-as -> aarch64-linux-gnu-ld -> qemu-aarch64. Output: "342" (len=3, head=42). exit=0.

fixtures/aarch64_list_smoke.oo:
  let mut xs: List[Int] = list_new();
  println(list_len(xs));

Pipeline (same as above). Output: "0". exit=0. Confirms list_new returns a real header and list_len reads [header+8].

== Tag and version ==

Tag v0.225.32 is anchored on commit abdf1e1d (Phase 2 commit). ooda.pkg version is 0.225.32. The previous tag v0.225.9 remains valid for the Tier 0 closure.

== Remaining gap to 11/10 ==

Phase 3 (no_output detector + shape_refuse gate) is next. Then Phase 5 (AST-path runtime smoke), Phase 6 (AAPCS64 multi-function-call smoke), Phase 7 (cross-arch output equivalence), Phase 8 (match/closure fixtures), Phase 9 (Tier 1 wiring). Phase 1 (chs_rt OoRes_S collision) stays cancelled as residual; the host-pipeline smokes prove the AArch64 backend is exercised without depending on the in-process ooda run path.
